<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xerxes I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 486–465 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Xerxes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xerxes I was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> from 486 to 465 BCE, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>. He led the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a> in 480 BCE, which won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> but was defeated at Salamis and, in 479 BCE, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>. Accounts of the Achaemenid Empire discuss him in the context of the empire's military history and the limits of its westward expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xerxes-i.jpg" alt="Xerxes I" width="278" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Darafsh · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Museum_of_Iran_Darafsh_(785).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Notable campaigns included Cyrus's conquest of Lydia, where camels were reportedly deployed to unsettle the Lydian cavalry, the conquest of Egypt (525 BCE), Darius's Scythian campaign (c. 513 BCE), and <b>Xerxes</b>'s massive invasion of Greece (480 BCE), which brought victory at Thermopylae and the sack of Athens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the urging of Themistocles the city then built a fleet of some 200 triremes, which formed the core of the Greek navy that defeated <b>Xerxes I</b> at the Battle of Salamis in 480 BCE, and Athenians fought at Plataea in 479 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Macedonia was never made a satrapy, but it supplied troops: Alexander I supported <b>Xerxes I</b> in 480–479 BCE, and Macedonians fought on the Persian side at the Battle of Plataea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 539 BCE the cities passed under the Achaemenid Empire, which they served for two centuries: their ships formed the core of the Persian fleet against the Greeks, and Herodotus rated the Sidonian ships the best in the fleet of <b>Xerxes I</b>, defeated at Salamis in 480 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Darius’s foundation charter at Susa records that the lapis lazuli and carnelian of his palace were brought from Sogdiana, and Herodotus lists the Sogdians among the peoples who paid tribute to the Persian king and marched with <b>Xerxes</b> against Greece.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Kingship could be dangerous: <b>Xerxes I</b> was murdered in his bed, and Artaxerxes III, Greek writers say, was poisoned by the eunuch Bagoas in 338 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li></ul></section>
